@packages/compiler/src/evaluation-registry.sigil import { SigilEvaluationSkillRegistry }

expand SigilEvaluationSkillRegistry {
  logic {
    EvaluationSkillPackage {
      Discover each built-in evaluation skill from its SKILL.md and compile.json
      package.

      Validate manifest identity, version, inputs, dependencies, capabilities, rules,
      implementation-evidence policy, and output contract before scheduling the
      skill.

      Supply the complete selected SKILL.md guidance to the adapter and bind accepted
      findings to the manifest's declared rule set.
    }

    ImplementationEvidencePolicy {
      Inject the selected skill's context-only or compare implementation-evidence
      policy into every evaluator request.

      Let context-only evaluators inspect implementation when it establishes a
      repository, platform, version, environment, or feasibility constraint.

      Let compare evaluators assess current implementation against desired Sigil.
    }

    UnifiedSemanticReadiness {
      Preserve the existing ambiguity, contradiction, missing-observable-behavior,
      and missing-rationale evaluation.

      Also assess goal responsibility and boundary clarity, materially applicable
      interface inputs, outputs, failures, side effects, lifecycle, ordering,
      authorization, retry, and compatibility behavior.

      Derive observable cases from material state and constraints and audit durable
      rationale coverage for material choices across every semantic section.

      Inspect the selected component, matching expands, direct dependencies,
      dependency decisions, and affected importers before reporting alignment.

      Run graph and selected context inspection when re-exposure, namespace
      ambiguity, ownership, or a suspected cross-Sigil contradiction makes the
      initially selected evidence insufficient.

      Distinguish a suspected problem requiring more evidence from a confirmed
      contradiction and report incomplete evidence rather than silently producing no
      finding.
    }

    ArchitectureModularityAndGraphHarmony {
      Evaluate the selected component as desired architecture through its matching
      expands, direct dependencies, direct importers, relevant module indexes, and
      accessible imported public namespace.

      Use targeted graph and context inspection for relationships and traverse
      farther only to confirm a dependency cycle, re-exposed concept, namespace
      ambiguity, ownership chain, transitive coupling, or boundary-summary
      disagreement.

      Assess responsibility cohesion, state and policy ownership, interface size,
      information hiding, coupling, dependency direction, reasons to change,
      component and expand decomposition, contract-to-implementation guidance,
      module-index scope, and imported namespace reuse.

      Let architecture-design declare ARCHITECTURE_BOUNDARY, COMPONENT_DECOMPOSITION,
      OWNERSHIP_BOUNDARY, DESIGN_COHESION, INTERFACE_BOUNDARY, COUPLING,
      DEPENDENCY_DIRECTION, DEPENDENCY_CYCLE, MODULE_INDEX_SCOPE, and
      IMPORTED_NAMESPACE_REUSE.

      Distinguish public-contract contradictions owned by semantic-readiness from
      ownership, dependency, decomposition, coupling, namespace, and change-locality
      findings owned by architecture-design.
    }
  }

  constraints {
    EvaluationSkillPackage {
      Hard-coded stage summaries do not replace packaged evaluation guidance.

      Missing, malformed, incompatible, or capability-mismatched required skill
      packages leave their stage incomplete.

      Evaluation skills cannot grant mutation, network, approval, or implementation
      authority.
    }

    ImplementationEvidencePolicy {
      context-only stages treat selected Sigil as the desired contract and do not
      report a finding solely because current implementation differs, is missing, or
      lacks ownership annotations.

      A context-only stage may use implementation evidence only when the resulting
      finding exists independently in Sigil or repository evidence demonstrates a
      genuine feasibility constraint rather than a different current structure.

      current-code-compatibility is the compare stage and exclusively owns
      implementation drift, missing implementation, ownership gaps, and current-code
      conformance.

      The manifest rule set remains an independent boundary and rejects diagnostic
      codes not declared by the selected skill.
    }

    UnifiedSemanticReadiness {
      Semantic readiness remains provisional evidence within the evaluated scope.

      Detailed cohesion, coupling, dependency-direction, and boundary classification
      belongs to architecture-design; semantic-readiness reports only the suspected
      issue and evidence needed when architectural analysis is required.

      Compatibility behavior in semantic-readiness means compatibility promised by
      the public contract rather than compatibility with current code.

      External standards research remains owned by standards-risk.

      ReviewGate, design conversation, workflow selection, concept grouping, glossary
      extraction, Sigil mutation, and implementation authorization remain outside
      compiler semantic readiness.
    }

    ArchitectureModularityAndGraphHarmony {
      Graph edges are evidence rather than findings by themselves.

      Architecture findings require a concrete ownership, dependency, coupling,
      decomposition, interface, namespace, or change-locality consequence.

      Architecture evaluation is qualitative and does not assign numeric modularity
      scores or report subjective style preferences.

      Each ModuleIndexFile remains a concise architectural summary and intentional
      namespace-assembly surface with one local boundary summary and imports for the
      cohesive components intended for shorthand.

      A module index matching expand retains only constraints and durable decisions
      that genuinely govern the whole indexed boundary.

      Material operational logic, mutable state, detailed lifecycle behavior, and
      independently changing policy belong to narrower components or expands unless
      they genuinely govern the whole indexed boundary.

      The component and expand decomposition must be specific enough to guide
      implementation into cohesive owning modules while an implementation index
      assembles the public namespace rather than owning unrelated behavior or state.

      Accessible semantically matching imported components and public concepts are
      reused before local identities; similar terminology with materially different
      responsibility does not force reuse.

      Current implementation structure may demonstrate a genuine feasibility
      constraint but is not authoritative desired architecture.
    }
  }

  decisions {
    EvaluationSkillLoading {
      Decision: Make versioned compiler skill packages the authoritative evaluator
      guidance and rule contract instead of hard-coded stage summaries.

      Scope: Governs built-in agentic compiler stages and adapter prompts; excludes
      host workflow skills and deterministic SigilCore validation.

      Trade-offs: Packaging, versioning, and manifest validation become required
      runtime responsibilities.

      Design issues addressed: Published SKILL.md and compile.json files cannot
      govern evaluation while runtime prompts ignore them.

      Consequences: Compiler behavior can be reviewed, tested, and versioned through
      the same package that declares its capabilities and findings.
    }

    UnifiedSemanticReadiness {
      Decision: Extend the compiler's existing readiness evaluator with the
      host-skill semantic checks that determine contract completeness while leaving
      workflow governance and detailed architecture evaluation in their existing
      owners.

      Scope: Governs the semantic-readiness compiler stage; excludes standards-risk,
      architecture-design classification, ReviewGate, authoring, and mutation.

      Trade-offs: The readiness package becomes more detailed and requires broader
      evidence fixtures.

      Design issues addressed: Separate host and compiler readiness checklists can
      produce materially different assessments from equivalent evidence.

      Discarded alternatives: Replacing compiler readiness with the complete host
      workflow and keeping two independent readiness authorities were rejected.

      Consequences: The compiler can be compared with current host readiness without
      duplicating approval or authoring workflows.

      Revisit when: Equivalence evaluations show that a check belongs in
      architecture-design or standards-risk instead.
    }

    ReadinessStageBoundary {
      Decision: Let semantic-readiness identify semantic completeness and suspected
      cross-boundary issues while architecture-design owns detailed modularity
      classification.

      Scope: Separates semantic-readiness from architecture-design and
      standards-risk.

      Trade-offs: Some suspected readiness findings require a later architecture
      stage for final classification.

      Consequences: The stages share evidence without issuing duplicate detailed
      findings.
    }

    StageEvidenceAuthority {
      Decision: Declare context-only or compare implementation-evidence authority in
      every evaluation skill manifest and inject that authority into evaluator
      instructions.

      Scope: Governs implementation evidence across compiler evaluation stages;
      excludes capability restrictions and current-code-compatibility rule details.

      Trade-offs: Prompt and manifest validation can constrain diagnostic authority
      but cannot deterministically prove that a model did not misclassify evidence
      under a generic allowed rule.

      Design issues addressed: Broad workspace traversal lets semantic and
      architecture evaluators observe current code and mistakenly report intentional
      design divergence as a defect.

      Discarded alternatives: Restricting traversal and creating duplicate design
      skills were rejected because repository facts remain useful evidence and the
      existing stages already own distinct questions.

      Consequences: Design evaluation can inspect implementation context without
      treating ordinary mismatch as drift, while current-code-compatibility retains
      exclusive comparison authority.
    }

    ArchitectureModularityAndGraphHarmony {
      Decision: Align architecture-design with the host Sigil skill's qualitative
      modularity, graph-harmony, ModuleIndexFile, and imported-namespace rules.

      Scope: Governs architecture-design findings for selected components, related
      contracts, graph neighborhoods, and module indexes; excludes public semantic
      contradiction and current implementation conformance.

      Trade-offs: Architecture evaluation requires broader targeted context and more
      precise diagnostic rules.

      Design issues addressed: Component-only cohesion checks cannot detect ownership
      overlap, cycles, module-index overreach, namespace duplication, or disharmony
      across related Sigil.

      Consequences: Architecture findings identify concrete structural problems while
      preserving the semantic-readiness and current-code-compatibility boundaries.
    }
  }

  cases {
    EvaluationSkillPackage {
      A packaged semantic-readiness skill is loaded with its declared rules before
      the first adapter invocation.

      A finding with a rule absent from compile.json cannot affect compilation color.

      A missing required SKILL.md leaves semantic-readiness incomplete.

      A missing or unknown implementation-evidence policy leaves its required stage
      incomplete.
    }

    UnifiedSemanticReadiness {
      A clear contract with no material gap produces no semantic-readiness finding.

      A material choice without reconstructable rationale produces a declared
      rationale-gap finding.

      A possible transitive contradiction causes direct graph and related-context
      inspection before it can become a confirmed error.

      An architectural cohesion concern is labeled suspected and delegated to
      architecture-design rather than duplicated as a detailed readiness finding.

      Current code that has not adopted a coherent desired contract produces no
      semantic-readiness finding solely for that mismatch.
    }

    ArchitectureModularityAndGraphHarmony {
      A component with unrelated state, policy, lifecycle, and reasons to change
      produces a component-decomposition finding.

      Two related contracts that claim the same mutable state produce an
      ownership-boundary finding.

      A dependency cycle confirmed through graph inspection produces a
      dependency-cycle finding.

      A ModuleIndexFile that owns narrower operational behavior produces a
      module-index-scope finding.

      Boundary-wide orchestration that cannot coherently belong to a narrower owner
      remains valid in its module index.

      A local public identity that duplicates an accessible semantically matching
      imported identity produces an imported-namespace-reuse finding.

      Current code organized differently from coherent desired Sigil produces no
      architecture finding without a genuine feasibility constraint.
    }
  }
}
